10:36:51 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is understandable to feel unsettled, but this does not mean school taught you nonsense or that you cannot trust anything else you learned. Many school explanations are **useful simplified models**. They are taught because they capture the main idea at the level students need, even though researchers would add exceptions and details. For example: - “The heart pumps blood” is true, though circulation is more complex than that sentence. - “Genes determine traits” is broadly true, though environment and many genes interact. - “You read the words on the page” is broadly true, though eye movements, attention, and prediction make the mechanism less uniform than it feels. That is not necessarily deception or “shakiness.” It is how teaching often works: start with a reliable core, then refine it when the distinctions become relevant. A good way to trust scientific claims is not to demand that every classroom sentence be perfectly exhaustive. Instead, ask: 1. **What is the core claim?** 2. **What conditions or exceptions apply?** 3. **Is the claim supported by multiple kinds of evidence and scientific consensus?** 4. **Does the new nuance contradict the core, or just make it more precise?** Here, the core remains intact: you normally read text accurately by processing visual information from words. The refinement is that the brain does not necessarily give every word identical attention or leave every word equally available to conscious report. That is a reason to see scientific knowledge as adjustable and self-correcting—not as something you must either accept completely or discard completely.
